-- allium: 4
-- Distilled behavioural spec of the LOAN ACCOUNTING subsystem: how a posted loan transaction
-- becomes balanced journal entries. This is the artefact a team maintains beside the code. It
-- speaks the domain, names NO implementation identifiers (no files, classes, methods), and is the
-- crystallised result of one past exploration. Modelled over the legs of one posted transaction.
--
-- The behavioural map (what a reader learns without touching the code):
--   * A posted loan transaction is split into TYPED LEGS: principal, interest, fee, penalty,
--     overpayment. Each leg posts a debit amount and a credit amount to the general ledger, chosen
--     by a per-type general-ledger account mapping.
--   * TWO POSTING REGIMES exist: cash basis and accrual basis. They differ in WHEN interest and
--     fees reach the ledger. On cash basis a leg posts when cash moves (at repayment). On accrual
--     basis interest and fees post when they ACCRUE, ahead of payment, and repayment then clears
--     the accrued receivable. Principal postings are the same shape on both.
--   * BALANCE IS EMERGENT, NOT ENFORCED. Over all legs of one posted transaction total debits equal
--     total credits, but nothing on the loan posting path asserts it. It holds only because each
--     regime's per-type branch is hand-written to post a matching debit and credit. A single leg is
--     one-sided; balance is invisible at any one posting site and only appears across all legs.
--   * The debit=credit assertion that DOES exist in the wider ledger guards a different entry route
--     (operator-submitted manual entries), not the loan-generated path. A loan-path leg added on
--     one side without its matching counterpart posts unbalanced, silently, with nothing to catch it.
--   * CHARGE-OFF repayment posting is the sharpest hazard: its two sides are accumulated separately
--     and never reconciled against each other.

component LoanPosting
  entity Leg                          -- the legs of one posted loan transaction

  observable state debit(Leg) : Money(gbp)      -- debit this leg posts to the general ledger
  observable state credit(Leg) : Money(gbp)     -- credit this leg posts to the general ledger

  -- No leg posts a negative amount to either side.
  invariant legs_nonneg means every l ::
    debit(l) >= 0 and credit(l) >= 0

  -- THE load-bearing invariant: over all legs of the transaction, total debits = total credits.
  invariant double_entry_balances means
    sum l :: debit(l) = sum l :: credit(l)

  -- A single leg may be one-sided (pure debit or pure credit); only the whole must balance.
  requirement leg_may_be_one_sided means some l ::
    debit(l) > 0 and credit(l) = 0
end
